You are building REALORA, a production-quality web application. Match the attached UI screenshots pixel-for-pixel in spirit and layout — same design language, same components, same copy where provided. This is not a landing page; it is a working two-sided real-estate platform (public marketplace + buyer portal + seller portal) with a shared demo dataset, a real deal state machine, and flows that connect the buyer and seller sides. Every button must do something. No lorem ipsum, no dead ends, no placeholder boxes.
1. What REALORA is (product rules — never violate these in UI or copy)
REALORA is an AI-native real-estate brokerage platform that represents both parties and orchestrates the entire transaction, from list to close, through AI agents.
Seller fee is 1% total (traditional NYC total is ~5.5%). The seller may optionally check a box to offer +1% to a buyer's agent who brings the buyer; REALORA's revenue stays 1%.
The platform is the hero. Never write copy that frames REALORA as "a better agent." Copy angle: the user does three things — set the price, choose the offer, sign — everything else is handled by the platform.
Market: NYC townhouses and condos, Brooklyn + Manhattan. Listings also syndicate to MLS/RLS portals in the real business; in this app, REALORA's own marketplace is the surface.
Buyers can buy direct with no buyer's agent ("Buy now" flow). Offers are structured term sheets (price, financing, down %, EMD, contingencies, close date) ranked for the seller by net proceeds and certainty, not price alone.
The platform never holds escrow money (in copy: deposits are held by the seller's attorney's escrow). Attorneys draft contracts; the platform coordinates everyone and everything.
Realora does not do tours. The platform does not conduct, host, staff, schedule, or book tours or showings of homes. No Realora person attends a viewing, ever. This is the model — a brokerage with no agent headcount doing in-person showings is a contradiction — and it is permanent: the absence of tour booking is by design, not a gap. Do not build it, do not spec it, do not leave UI placeholders or copy that promise it, and do not treat any tour-shaped hole in the data as a TODO.
Keep the demo-honesty labels from the screenshots where they appear ("demo listing", "Demo preview", "Demo estimate only — not an appraisal or acceptance guarantee.", "Prototype decisions only").
2. Tech stack (use exactly this unless impossible)
Next.js 15 (App Router) + TypeScript (strict) + Tailwind CSS v4 (CSS-first configuration: @import "tailwindcss" + @theme inline design tokens — the canonical globals.css is provided in §3; start from it, extend it, never replace it).
Font: Plus Jakarta Sans via next/font/google, exposed as the CSS variable --font-plus-jakarta and used for everything — wordmark, headings, body, UI.
shadcn/ui primitives allowed for dialogs/drawers/inputs, but restyle them to the design system below — the app must NOT look like default shadcn.
Framer Motion for subtle motion (page fades, card entrances, modal/drawer transitions, animated progress bars and number count-ups). Motion is quiet and premium, never bouncy.
Lucide icons only (the screenshots use Lucide-style line icons).
Map: the reference build renders the marketplace map as a static map image (a Manhattan street-map screenshot/export) recolored with the .realora-map-image CSS filter from §3, with price pins positioned absolutely by percentage coordinates from the seed data. Reproduce that approach — it is pixel-stable, needs no API key, and matches the screenshots. (A live MapLibre layer is an acceptable later upgrade; it is not required and must not change the visual result.)
State: a typed demo store (React Context + useReducer, or Zustand) seeded from /src/data/seed.ts, persisted to localStorage, with a Reset demo action that restores the seed exactly.
No backend. Put every external integration behind a typed service interface in /src/lib/services/ with a mock implementation (auth, payments/Stripe, e-sign, ID verification, ACRIS, RLS syndication, vendors) so real APIs can replace mocks without touching UI.
